Utilities for Raspberry Pi
==========================

About the Boot Partition
------------------------

The Raspberry Pi boots from an SD or micro-SD card.  The card includes a
small boot partition plus a larger main partition.  While the main partition
uses one of the Linux-only 'ext' file systems, the boot partition is a vfat
file system, so it can be read and written by Windows, Mac, and Linux systems.
On current Raspberry Pi OS it's mounted at /boot/firmware (formerly /boot).

DerbyNet keeps its Pi settings in a derbynet.conf file on the boot partition.
That makes it possible to change what a Pi does without bringing a keyboard,
mouse, and monitor: with the Pi powered off, put the SD card in a Windows, Mac,
or Linux machine, edit derbynet.conf, then put the card back in the Pi and
power it on.  (Network settings such as WiFi are set in Raspberry Pi Imager
when the card is written, not here.)


Setting Up a Pi for DerbyNet
----------------------------

setup.sh turns a fresh Raspberry Pi OS install into a DerbyNet Pi that can be
any combination of:

  server    the DerbyNet web server
  standby   a warm spare that keeps a copy of the server's data (at most about
            40 seconds old)
  kiosk     a full-screen kiosk: a display, a track camera (USB webcam or Pi
            camera) or a photo stand (camera plus barcode scanner), whichever
            the Kiosk Dashboard makes it

A kiosk needs Raspberry Pi OS with desktop.  Lite is enough
for a Pi that will only ever be a server or standby, but can't later take on
the other roles.  On the Pi, as the normal user:

    sudo apt install -y git        # if git isn't already installed
    git clone https://github.com/redshedjed/derbynet.git
    bash derbynet/extras/rpi/setup.sh --roles "server kiosk"
    sudo reboot

setup.sh installs everything for every role, so changing roles later doesn't
need re-running it, or internet access: edit ROLES in derbynet.conf on the
boot partition and reboot (or run "sudo derbynet-pi apply").  The
derbynet.conf that setup.sh installs documents all the settings.  Re-run
setup.sh to deploy updated website code from the checkout.


Finding the Server
------------------

The server announces itself on the local network (as a DNS-SD service, like a
network printer does), and the other Pis find it automatically; nothing needs
to be configured on them.  The server also publishes an alias, so people's
laptops and phones can browse to

    http://derbynet.local/derbynet

The server's own hostname isn't changed.  Some Android devices don't resolve
.local names; use the server's IP address there ("derbynet-pi status" on the
server shows it).

If the server isn't a Pi set up this way (e.g. it's a laptop), set
DERBYNET_SERVER in derbynet.conf on each Pi instead.


Standby and Failover
--------------------

For a standby, set the same SYNC_PASSWORD in derbynet.conf on both the server
and the standby; without it, the standby can't copy anything.

If the server fails, power it off, then on the standby run

    sudo derbynet-pi promote

The standby starts announcing itself and takes over the alias.  Kiosks (displays,
track cameras and photo stands) notice that the old server has stopped answering
and switch to the new one on their own, within about a minute.  promote
refuses to run while the old server is still announcing itself.

Before powering the old server back on, change its ROLES (e.g. to standby) so
the two don't both act as the server.


Checking on a Pi
----------------

    derbynet-pi status     what this Pi is doing, and whether it's healthy
    derbynet-pi doctor     status, plus hardware checks (cameras, scanners)
    derbynet-pi servers    DerbyNet servers announced on the network
